artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a forest-based supply chain at the tactical planning horizon (typically several months to a year). The supply chain consists of a finite set of stakeholders S = {1, ..., n}, each representing a distinct decision-making entity such as a forest owner, a sawmill or pulp mill, a transportation company, or another business unit. Each stakeholder i ∈ S controls a private decision vector x_i ∈ X_i (representing harvest schedules, production plans, transport flows, inventory levels, etc.) and seeks to optimize its own objective function f_i(x_i, x_{-i}), where x_{-i} denotes the decisions of the other stakeholders. The stakeholders are linked by physical flow constraints (e.g., wood volumes leaving forests must match volumes arriving at mills, transport capacities are shared) and by contractual or market relationships, but no central authority can dictate a fully integrated solution because the stakeholders have different and possibly conflicting objectives, private information, and independent ownership.

Develop a tactical-level coordination methodology — for instance, a pricing scheme, an auction or market-based mechanism, a contract design, or a decomposition/negotiation protocol — that:
(a) respects the autonomy and information privacy of each stakeholder (each i decides x_i based only on locally available information plus signals exchanged through the mechanism);
(b) synchronizes the decisions x_1, ..., x_n in time and quantity so that the resulting plans are jointly feasible with respect to the shared physical and capacity constraints;
(c) provably improves a global supply-chain performance measure F(x_1, ..., x_n) (e.g., total profit, total cost, service level, or environmental indicator) compared with a baseline of uncoordinated independent optimization, ideally with quantifiable bounds on the loss relative to the fully integrated centralized optimum.

Specify the mechanism precisely, prove its incentive and convergence properties under stated assumptions on f_i and X_i, and characterize the conditions under which it yields a coordinated tactical plan superior to non-cooperative behavior.

